1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:18,200
In the last module you learned a language. In this one you build something you can touch.
This is the longest module in the course, and it's the one where things start to feel like
real app development.

2
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:24,200
Here is what you will be able to do by the end of this video.

3
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:40,200
UIKit is Apple's long-established interface framework. It's built on views, view
controllers, and an event-driven model: the user does something, your code responds. Screens
are laid out in a storyboard, a visual file where you drag controls onto a canvas.

4
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:48,600
Before layout, though, we need to talk about where your code goes. Because this is where
most beginner projects go wrong.

5
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:59,800
The pattern is MVVM — Model, View, ViewModel. Three responsibilities. The model holds data.
The view model owns logic and formatting. The view only displays what it's handed.

6
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:15,800
Here's a real view model from the app. Look at what's inside it: the search filtering, the
category filtering, the rating sort. All of that logic lives here — and not one line of it
is in the view controller.

7
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:35,800
Why bother? Two reasons. The first is that the view controller stays small and readable
instead of becoming a dumping ground for everything. The second matters more than it sounds:
logic in a view model can be tested. Logic buried inside a screen cannot. We'll prove that

8
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:46,600
The rule for the rest of the course: No data handling, no parsing, no network calls in the
view. All of it goes in the view model.

9
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:57,000
Next: the view controller life cycle. Every screen moves through a fixed sequence of methods
as it appears and disappears. Two of them you'll use constantly.

10
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:19,800
ViewDidLoad runs once, when the screen first loads. That's where setup goes. viewWillAppear
runs every single time the screen is about to be shown. So if you need fresh data each time
the user comes back to a screen, that's the one you want. Putting it in the wrong place is a

11
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:31,000
Auto Layout. This is the part almost everyone finds hardest. If you struggle here, you are
exactly where everybody else is. It isn't conceptually deep, it's just fiddly.

12
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:48,600
The reason it exists is history. Apple originally shipped one iPhone size, so layout was
trivial. Once there were Pro models, Max models, SE models and iPads, they needed a system
that could scale one design across all of them. That system is constraints.

13
00:02:48,600 --> 00:03:06,200
Two pieces of vocabulary. The origin — zero, zero — is the top-left corner. And constraints
use 'leading' and 'trailing' rather than left and right, because they follow reading
direction. That means the same layout works in a right-to-left language without you changing

14
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:23,800
And here's the technique that makes constraints manageable: stack views. This seat map is
rows of seats inside a vertical stack. Rather than constraining every individual seat
button, you constrain the stack, and it handles what's inside. One set of constraints

15
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:36,200
If you get stuck, don't let constraints block you. Build the working flow first, treat the
layout polish as a finishing pass, and group things into stacks before you start
constraining.

16
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:40,600
Moving between screens. There are three ways; two are worth using.

17
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:55,400
Push navigation slides the next screen in from the right and gives you a back button
automatically. Modal presentation slides up from the bottom and sits on top — good for an
about screen or a dialog.

18
00:03:55,400 --> 00:04:13,800
The third is a segue, drawn in the storyboard with no code. It works, but it constrains you:
segues force you through the hierarchy, so if you're five screens deep and need to jump back
to screen two, a segue can't. Doing navigation in code can.

19
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:31,000
One gotcha that will cost you an afternoon. Push navigation does nothing at all unless your
screen sits inside a navigation controller. The code is correct, the button does nothing,
and there's no error message. If that happens to you — that's why.

20
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:47,000
Finally, table views. A table view is a single scrolling column of rows, like a social media
feed. A collection view is a grid of tiles, like a photo gallery. Table views are far more
common, so that's our focus.

21
00:04:47,000 --> 00:05:00,200
Two protocols make one work. The data source supplies content: how many rows, and what's in
each. The delegate handles interaction: what happens when a row is tapped. You assign both
in viewDidLoad.

22
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:11,000
Miss that assignment and the table renders completely empty — with no error to tell you why.
It's probably the single most common beginner stumble in UIKit.

23
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:27,800
One more detail in this cell. prepareForReuse. Table views recycle cells as you scroll —
that's what makes them fast. But it means a cell can arrive still holding the previous row's
image. Clearing it on reuse is what prevents that flicker.

24
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:47,400
When you type in the search bar or flip the rating sort, the screen isn't doing that work.
It's asking the view model for a filtered, sorted list and displaying the result. The rows
are reusable cells built from stack views. The whole module is on screen at once.

25
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:49,900
Let's recap the key points.

26
00:05:49,900 --> 00:05:52,400
Here is your practice exercise.

27
00:05:52,400 --> 00:06:04,000
Module three steps back into the language for the intermediate features — closures,
collections and concurrency — and then covers how to plan an app before you write it.
